partments with Elevated Breach Rates\nThe following departments have breach rates above the threshold, but not exceeding 10%:\n\n| Department | Breach Rate | Formal Complaints |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| General Medicine | **0.2857142857142857** | 1 |\n| Emergency | 0.2 | 2 |\n| Dermatology | 0.16666666666666666 | 2 |\n| Paediatrics | 0.125 | 2 |\n| Orthopaedics | 0.1 | 2 |\n\n## Policy Insights\nAccording to policy, departments with breach rates exceeding **10%** may be placed on a Performance Improvement Plan (PIP). The current breach rates for the listed departments are:\n\n* Radiology: **50%**\n* Cardiology: **37.5%**\n* Oncology: **33.33%**\n* Neurology: **33.33%**\n\n## Recommended Action\nReview and address the SLA wait time breaches in the identified departments to ensure timely care for VIP and Premium tier patients. Implement corrective actions, such as additional staffing or process improvements, to prevent future breaches and maintain high-quality patient care standards.\nUSER: Which clinical departments are exhibiting the worst SLA wait time breach rates for VIP and Premium tier patients, and how many of those breaches resulted in formal patient complaints?\nASSISTANT: ## Executive Summary\n**High SLA Breach Rates Identified Across Multiple Departments**\n\nOur analysis reveals that several clinical departments have breached Service Level Agreements (SLAs) for VIP and Premium tier patients. These breaches may impact the quality of care provided to these high-priority patients.\n\n### Departmental Performance\n\n| Department | Breach Rate (%) | Formal Complaints |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Radiology | **50%** | 1 |\n| Cardiology | **37.5%** | **3** |\n| Oncology | **33.3%** | 3 |\n| Neurology | **33.3%** | 0 |\n| General Medicine | **28.6%** | 1 |\n| Emergency | **20%** | 2 |\n| Dermatology | **16.7%** | 2 |\n| Paediatrics | **12.5%** | 2 |\n| Orthopaedics | **10%** | 2 |\n| Gynaecology | **0%** | 0 |\n\n### Policy Insights\n\nDepartments with breach rates e